WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:08.428
Begin by framing Program Overview and Status
as a question about inputs, decisions, and

2
00:00:08.428 --> 00:00:09.625
observable results.

3
00:00:09.625 --> 00:00:17.109
When registered for co-op, students are
considered by RIT to have full-time status.

4
00:00:17.109 --> 00:00:25.874
Consider this worked case: A student enrolled
in CSCI-499 for the fall semester is counted

5
00:00:25.874 --> 00:00:31.718
as a full-time undergraduate for financial aid
and housing purposes.

6
00:00:31.718 --> 00:00:38.791
List the givens before choosing a method,
because an unstated assumption can change the

7
00:00:38.791 --> 00:00:39.296
conclusion.

8
00:00:39.296 --> 00:00:45.015
This establishes the vocabulary and scope
needed for the remaining sections.

9
00:00:45.122 --> 00:00:51.970
Now define Time Commitment Requirements
precisely enough that another learner could

10
00:00:51.970 --> 00:00:53.837
test the definition.

11
00:00:53.837 --> 00:01:00.353
In order to register for co-op for either fall
or spring semester, we expect that students

12
00:01:00.353 --> 00:01:06.868
will work a minimum of 14 weeks and work a
minimum of 35 hours per week.

13
00:01:06.868 --> 00:01:14.198
Consider this worked case: A student planning
a spring co-op must schedule at least 490

14
00:01:14.198 --> 00:01:17.618
hours of work spread across the semester.

15
00:01:17.618 --> 00:01:23.886
Separate the formal definition from an
implementation detail, then identify what

16
00:01:23.886 --> 00:01:26.165
evidence would distinguish them.

17
00:01:26.165 --> 00:01:32.571
A precise definition makes later examples
easier to evaluate and revise.

18
00:01:32.690 --> 00:01:40.406
With the foundation in place, examine how
Prerequisite Coursework operates in a concrete

19
00:01:40.406 --> 00:01:40.999
sequence.

20
00:01:40.999 --> 00:01:47.927
Students must complete a student co-op work
report for each term for which they are

21
00:01:47.927 --> 00:01:52.546
registered; students are also evaluated each
term by their supervisor.

22
00:01:52.546 --> 00:02:00.861
Consider this worked case: A student who has
not taken CSCI-099 must enroll in it first to

23
00:02:00.861 --> 00:02:03.796
become eligible for the co-op program.

24
00:02:03.796 --> 00:02:10.952
Follow every arrow in order and name the state
before and after each transition.

25
00:02:10.952 --> 00:02:16.780
The trace converts an abstract statement into
an inspectable sequence.

26
00:02:16.898 --> 00:02:24.004
Next, compare the choices involved in Student
Work Report Requirement and make the decision

27
00:02:24.004 --> 00:02:25.019
criteria explicit.

28
00:02:25.019 --> 00:02:33.261
A satisfactory grade is given for co-op when
both a completed student co-op work report and

29
00:02:33.261 --> 00:02:38.412
a completed, corresponding supervisor
evaluation are received and when both

30
00:02:38.412 --> 00:02:40.473
documents are generally consistent.

31
00:02:40.473 --> 00:02:48.072
Consider this worked case: A student working
on a software development project must submit

32
00:02:48.072 --> 00:02:52.957
a written report detailing their tasks,
challenges, and outcomes.

33
00:02:52.957 --> 00:03:00.162
Compare alternatives against the same
constraints so the evaluation does not move

34
00:03:00.162 --> 00:03:01.363
the goalposts.

35
00:03:01.363 --> 00:03:08.176
Using shared criteria turns a preference into
a defensible technical choice.

36
00:03:08.282 --> 00:03:16.031
Apply Supervisor Evaluation Requirement to a
worked situation instead of treating it as an

37
00:03:16.031 --> 00:03:17.138
isolated definition.

38
00:03:17.138 --> 00:03:21.997
Students perform professional work related to
Computer Science.

39
00:03:21.997 --> 00:03:28.865
Consider this worked case: A supervisor
reviews the student's code commits and

40
00:03:28.865 --> 00:03:32.872
deliverables to provide a formal performance
evaluation.

41
00:03:32.872 --> 00:03:38.403
'Supervisor Evaluation' with a checkmark
indicating consistency.

42
00:03:38.403 --> 00:03:45.859
Work from the concrete inputs to the result,
checking units, types, or invariants at each

43
00:03:45.859 --> 00:03:46.356
step.

44
00:03:46.356 --> 00:03:51.153
The worked case shows both how to apply the
method and how to check it.

45
00:03:51.153 --> 00:03:58.245
Name each dependency explicitly, and keep
labels and representations consistent

46
00:03:58.245 --> 00:04:00.372
throughout the analysis.

47
00:04:00.482 --> 00:04:07.716
Examine the tradeoffs in Grading Consistency
Standard, including what improves and what

48
00:04:07.716 --> 00:04:09.525
becomes more difficult.

49
00:04:09.525 --> 00:04:15.619
Robust security should be designed and
implemented when appropriate.

50
00:04:15.619 --> 00:04:23.545
Consider this worked case: If the student
report describes a database migration but the

51
00:04:23.545 --> 00:04:30.338
supervisor evaluation focuses solely on UI
testing, the grade is not satisfactory.

52
00:04:30.338 --> 00:04:36.978
State the benefit, the cost, and the condition
under which the tradeoff is acceptable.

53
00:04:36.978 --> 00:04:44.541
Making the tradeoff visible prevents a local
improvement from hiding a larger cost.

54
00:04:44.666 --> 00:04:51.413
Stress-test the reasoning around Ethical
Computing Principles with an edge case and

55
00:04:51.413 --> 00:04:54.787
identify which assumption controls the
outcome.

56
00:04:54.787 --> 00:05:01.537
Professionals should strive to achieve high
quality in both processes and products.

57
00:05:01.537 --> 00:05:09.722
Consider this worked case: A co-op student
developing an AI model must audit training

58
00:05:09.722 --> 00:05:15.569
data for bias and document limitations to
prevent harmful outcomes.

59
00:05:15.569 --> 00:05:22.162
Change one assumption at a time and observe
which part of the reasoning no longer holds.

60
00:05:22.162 --> 00:05:29.272
The edge case reveals the boundary of the
claim without discarding the useful core idea.

61
00:05:29.378 --> 00:05:36.903
Use Responsible Research Communication to
diagnose a failure by separating symptoms,

62
00:05:36.903 --> 00:05:39.640
causes, and corrective action.

63
00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:45.781
Responsible technical communication
distinguishes claims from evidence.

64
00:05:45.781 --> 00:05:53.028
Consider this worked case: A student's co-op
report must include a literature review

65
00:05:53.028 --> 00:05:58.046
section that cites existing algorithms before
presenting new results.

66
00:05:58.046 --> 00:06:05.741
Locate the first point where observed behavior
diverges from expected behavior, then test the

67
00:06:05.741 --> 00:06:07.390
smallest plausible cause.

68
00:06:07.390 --> 00:06:14.765
The diagnosis is strongest when another person
can reproduce the observation and the test.

69
00:06:14.882 --> 00:06:22.183
Connect Research Integrity and Transparency to
the earlier ideas and explain how the pieces

70
00:06:22.183 --> 00:06:24.269
work as one system.

71
00:06:24.269 --> 00:06:29.503
Limitations and threats to validity should be
made visible.

72
00:06:29.503 --> 00:06:36.901
Consider this worked case: A co-op project
report must include a dedicated section

73
00:06:36.901 --> 00:06:43.160
listing data collection constraints and
potential biases in the experimental setup.

74
00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:52.642
Explain each connection explicitly and verify
that information retains the same meaning

75
00:06:52.642 --> 00:06:54.222
across representations.

76
00:06:54.222 --> 00:07:01.785
The synthesis matters only if every connection
remains supported by the original evidence.

77
00:07:01.898 --> 00:07:08.880
Finish with Practical Analysis Checklist as a
practical checklist that can guide a new

78
00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:09.379
analysis.

79
00:07:09.379 --> 00:07:16.268
They should maintain high standards of
professional competence, conduct, and ethical

80
00:07:16.268 --> 00:07:16.894
practice.

81
00:07:16.894 --> 00:07:23.215
Consider this worked case: Before final
submission, a student checks that their

82
00:07:23.215 --> 00:07:29.537
14-week schedule is logged, their report
matches the supervisor's evaluation, and all

83
00:07:29.537 --> 00:07:31.644
ethical guidelines are met.

84
00:07:31.644 --> 00:07:39.905
For each checklist item, record the evidence,
the remaining uncertainty, and the next

85
00:07:39.905 --> 00:07:41.176
verification step.

86
00:07:41.176 --> 00:07:48.161
The result is a repeatable procedure, not a
conclusion that depends on memory or

87
00:07:48.161 --> 00:07:48.660
intuition.
